we're talking about this 25 years since  the release of pieces  of you what do you recall most about  that time in your life  gosh i remember  uh  i don't even know how to sum it up um  you know i moved out when i was 15 and i  didn't want to be a statistic i grew up  in an abusive household and i knew kids  like me  you know if you move out especially at  such a young age you can be a statistic  really easily and repeat the cycle  and i didn't want to be a statistic and  three short years later  i was homeless you know i ended up  homeless because i wouldn't have sex  with a boss  and started living in my car because he  wouldn't pay me my for my rim  and then my car got stolen and uh  i just remember it was like this moment  where it was a transformational time  this was either going to  kill me or i was going to learn and i  ended up learning  and making a really honest record i got  discovered while i was homeless and  i almost didn't sign the deal which  sounds really strange  but i had just started figuring out what  happiness was even though i was homeless  and i remember being on the beach and  making myself a promise that my number  one job would always be to figure out  how to be happy  and a whole human not a human full of  holes  and that my number two job would be to  be a musician and  that in my music i would always choose  art over  fame and with those two promises i  decided to go ahead and sign this record  contract and it led me  on a wild ride that is still going today  but the thing i think i'm most proud of  looking back is that moment on the beach  when i made myself those two promises  and that's what i really think about  yeah people talk a lot about the  pitfalls of fame  and how that's at this is important to  keep in mind um  when you went from making that promise  to yourself on the beach to  all of a sudden everybody knowing your  name what was it like getting that  attention was it overwhelming  it was very overwhelming for me and my  